WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en-GB

00:00:00.520 --> 00:00:01.520
Welcome and Opening Comments
Malcolm Grant

00:00:01.520 --> 00:00:02.520
Chairman
Preamble

00:00:02.520 --> 00:00:03.520
Transparency
Good morning, everybody, and welcome to this

00:00:03.520 --> 00:00:05.940
meeting of the board of NHS England.

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:12.400
I particularly welcome those who have joined
us here in Newbury this morning for the meeting,

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:16.700
and you are aware also that the meeting is
being web streamed.

00:00:16.700 --> 00:00:22.840
It is a meeting that we hold in public but
it is not a public meeting; this is a business

00:00:22.840 --> 00:00:24.190
meeting of the board.

00:00:24.190 --> 00:00:32.640
I should just assure you that we do our business
wholly committed to the principles of openness

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:36.860
and transparency, because we believe that
it is right that the people of England should

00:00:36.860 --> 00:00:43.000
see how decisions are taken about this extraordinary,
valuable social institution, the NHS, and

00:00:43.000 --> 00:00:51.000
the way we determine the allocation of resources,
of the £96 billion budget that is entrusted

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:55.610
to us across the provision of services to
the population of England.

00:00:55.610 --> 00:00:58.820
However, we do not do all of our business
in these public meetings.

00:00:58.820 --> 00:01:03.740
After this meeting we will go into private
session, because there are two or three items

00:01:03.740 --> 00:01:09.760
of business that are still in the process
of being determined by us, before we bring

00:01:09.760 --> 00:01:13.330
them back to a future public meeting for final
deliberations.

00:01:13.330 --> 00:01:17.819
Development session
Also, yesterday -- as is commonly the case

00:01:17.819 --> 00:01:23.260
with all of our meetings -- we all came together
with our sleeves rolled up in a 'development

00:01:23.260 --> 00:01:29.490
session' and think through some major issues
for future development of strategy, future

00:01:29.490 --> 00:01:30.490
development of policy.

00:01:30.490 --> 00:01:37.579
So, for example, yesterday we had a briefing
session around some particular aspects of

00:01:37.579 --> 00:01:44.060
direct commissioning; for example, in relation
to offender health and the health and justice

00:01:44.060 --> 00:01:49.259
relationship, and in relation to military
health and the services that we provide to

00:01:49.259 --> 00:01:50.259
the armed forces.

00:01:50.259 --> 00:01:57.289
We also had a valuable session yesterday on
one of the key areas for future development

00:01:57.289 --> 00:02:05.749
by us, which is around mental health and parity
of esteem, which we are committed to developing

00:02:05.749 --> 00:02:10.530
between recognition of mental health and physical
health.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:15.700
So that is something by way of background
to the formal deliberations that we will be

00:02:15.700 --> 00:02:27.170
undertaking this morning.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:42.130
Formal business
Apologies for absence

00:02:42.130 --> 00:02:48.260
I have, sadly, apologies this morning from
Rosamond Roughton, Naguib Kheraj and Bill

00:02:48.260 --> 00:02:54.320
McCarthy, all three of whom were with us yesterday,
but also from Moira Gibb, who is currently

00:02:54.320 --> 00:02:55.320
abroad.

00:02:55.320 --> 00:03:00.160
Declarations of interest
I would then like to ask any members of the

00:03:00.160 --> 00:03:04.330
board whether there are any declarations of
interest in any particular matters on the

00:03:04.330 --> 00:03:05.450
agenda today.

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:10.430
There is of course a statutory register of
declarations of interest, which is our standing

00:03:10.430 --> 00:03:15.670
register, but if anybody has any particular
interests that are arising today I would ask

00:03:15.670 --> 00:03:17.340
for those to be reported now.

00:03:17.340 --> 00:03:19.000
No, we have not.

00:03:19.000 --> 00:03:23.300
Minutes of last meeting
We then take the minutes of the last meeting.

00:03:23.300 --> 00:03:25.330
Can I take those as a fair and accurate record?

00:03:25.330 --> 00:03:26.670
New Chief Executive
Appointment of Simon Stevens

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:32.770
May I then make a report on one item, and
that is on the appointment of a new chief

00:03:32.770 --> 00:03:42.120
executive to NHS England.

00:03:42.120 --> 00:03:49.140
Many months ago, we received with regret a
report from our existing chief executive of

00:03:49.140 --> 00:03:54.740
his intention to retire with effect from the
1st April 2014.

00:03:54.740 --> 00:04:01.340
We launched a search for a successor to the
Chief Executive.

00:04:01.340 --> 00:04:06.590
That was a search in which we engaged head
hunters to work with us and for us.

00:04:06.590 --> 00:04:11.430
We undertook a rigorous appraisal of potential
candidates.

00:04:11.430 --> 00:04:19.039
Not from within the NHS solely and not from
within the UK solely, but a global search.

00:04:19.039 --> 00:04:25.749
As a consequence of that process, we were
delighted to announce two weeks ago the appointment

00:04:25.749 --> 00:04:32.520
of Simon Stevens to be the Chief Executive
of NHS England, with effect from 1st April

00:04:32.520 --> 00:04:33.520
2014.

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:37.909
The statutory arrangement for this, by the
way, is that the appointment is made by the

00:04:37.909 --> 00:04:44.199
non-executive members of NHS England -- that
is, those of us who do not hold an executive

00:04:44.199 --> 00:04:49.810
position within the organisation -- and it
requires the consent of the Secretary of State

00:04:49.810 --> 00:04:51.060
for Health.

00:04:51.060 --> 00:04:56.150
So I am pleased to report this morning that
that consent has been received, and that subject

00:04:56.150 --> 00:05:03.620
to any final details surrounding the appointment,
we look forward to welcoming Simon Stevens

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:06.800
in taking up the post with effect from next
year.

00:05:06.800 --> 00:05:10.289
Retirement of David Nicholson
I think two things remain to be said.

00:05:10.289 --> 00:05:15.979
The first is that an organisation can only
ever have one chief executive at a time, and

00:05:15.979 --> 00:05:22.509
until the 1st April 2014, David Nicholson
remains the Chief Executive of the organisation.

00:05:22.509 --> 00:05:28.909
And I know that we would wish again this morning
to formally extend our gratitude to David

00:05:28.909 --> 00:05:34.189
for everything that he has done to bring NHS
England into existence, and to bring us through

00:05:34.189 --> 00:05:39.199
a very difficult transitional time in setting
up the new organisation.

00:05:39.199 --> 00:05:45.729
This is not the time to wish David well for
the future, because there will be other opportunities

00:05:45.729 --> 00:05:47.349
for us to do that.

00:05:47.349 --> 00:05:52.190
However, secondly, David and the rest of us
will be working closely behind the scenes

00:05:52.190 --> 00:05:57.389
with Simon Stevens over the coming months
to ensure that the transition is as smooth

00:05:57.389 --> 00:06:03.400
as possible and that his leadership starts
from the 1st April with the full backing of

00:06:03.400 --> 00:06:17.039
the board and with a full and developed understanding
of the challenges that lie ahead of us.

00:06:17.039 --> 00:06:19.970
Chief Executive's Report
David Nicholson

00:06:19.970 --> 00:06:20.970
Chief Executive
Strategy

00:06:20.970 --> 00:06:25.189
You will have seen a copy of the report.

00:06:25.189 --> 00:06:31.289
I particularly want to concentrate on one
issue in the report.

00:06:31.289 --> 00:06:40.300
That is the issue of strategy: the issue that
is facing us about how we can create a sustainable

00:06:40.300 --> 00:06:46.379
NHS going forward into the medium term, which
is capable of delivering our objectives of

00:06:46.379 --> 00:06:53.080
universal coverage, free at the point of use,
but within a constrained financial envelope.

00:06:53.080 --> 00:07:00.369
We have reported before the detailed work
that NHS England has done on this, and this

00:07:00.369 --> 00:07:03.860
is a unique position, I think, for us as an
organisation.

00:07:03.860 --> 00:07:10.430
In a sense, we were created to give this opportunity
to think in the medium- and long-term about

00:07:10.430 --> 00:07:21.229
the NHS, irrespective of the electoral timetables
or whatever, as an independent board, thinking

00:07:21.229 --> 00:07:26.449
with our colleagues across the NHS and social
care system about how we can develop and sustain

00:07:26.449 --> 00:07:29.120
the NHS going forward.

00:07:29.120 --> 00:07:32.529
Hence the development of the call for action.

00:07:32.529 --> 00:07:37.199
Huge amounts of work are going on around all
of that; around the country people are having

00:07:37.199 --> 00:07:38.199
discussions.

00:07:38.199 --> 00:07:44.439
There has been a series of national conferences
and debates about the future of the NHS.

00:07:44.439 --> 00:07:51.189
We are working very closely with the other
arms-length bodies -- Monitor, the National

00:07:51.189 --> 00:07:56.569
Health Service Trust Development Authority,
CQC, and others -- to ensure that we have

00:07:56.569 --> 00:08:00.689
a connected position across all of the country.

00:08:00.689 --> 00:08:02.319
So that is going on.

00:08:02.319 --> 00:08:10.490
In a sense, where all of this ends up is a
series of local plans which identify what

00:08:10.490 --> 00:08:18.330
the NHS and social care system in individual
areas will look like in five years' time,

00:08:18.330 --> 00:08:23.749
but also particularly for the next two years,
in detail, with financial and operational

00:08:23.749 --> 00:08:28.719
plans that show how local people are going
to improve outcomes for patients and at the

00:08:28.719 --> 00:08:30.639
same time live within their financial constraints.

00:08:30.639 --> 00:08:33.500
So that is a really important thing.

00:08:33.500 --> 00:08:39.470
We are not here planning to produce some great
big national tome that sets out an NHS strategy

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:41.780
or anything of that nature.

00:08:41.780 --> 00:08:45.140
We believe the NHS is local, and that is where
it should be done.

00:08:45.140 --> 00:08:47.370
Interventions
However, there are some interventions that

00:08:47.370 --> 00:08:50.570
we as a board, we as NHS England, need to
make during that period.

00:08:50.570 --> 00:08:56.750
And we are starting to see those come to fruition.

00:08:56.750 --> 00:09:04.290
The first one is that we said that we would
produce a strategy for urgent and emergency

00:09:04.290 --> 00:09:12.940
care, which Bruce has been leading, and that
is due to be published later this month.

00:09:12.940 --> 00:09:16.930
Bruce will undoubtedly talk about that later
in the meeting.

00:09:16.930 --> 00:09:22.470
That is a really important and radical plan
for changes in urgent and emergency care.

00:09:22.470 --> 00:09:30.560
We are also planning to do a significant amount
of work on what we would describe as 'Any

00:09:30.560 --> 00:09:32.260
Town CCG'.

00:09:32.260 --> 00:09:39.630
What we want to do is to pose the question:
if we implemented all of the really significant

00:09:39.630 --> 00:09:44.850
improvements for outcomes for patients in
one place at the same time, what would the

00:09:44.850 --> 00:09:49.960
impact be on the finances and services in
an average CCG?

00:09:49.960 --> 00:09:55.600
We think that will be a really useful and
practical way of turning some of the broader

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:00.880
ideas people have about change into some specifics
for a local population.

00:10:00.880 --> 00:10:06.380
That work is ongoing and we plan to publish
that soon.

00:10:06.380 --> 00:10:13.180
We have also said that we want to think about
what the future of provision might look like.

00:10:13.180 --> 00:10:17.360
It is not our job to provide healthcare; we
are commissioners.

00:10:17.360 --> 00:10:23.950
However, working with Monitor, we have set
up a symposium later this month where we are

00:10:23.950 --> 00:10:29.650
bringing experts together to think about,
over the next five and ten years, what providers

00:10:29.650 --> 00:10:32.840
of the future will look like, what general
practice will look like, what hospitals will

00:10:32.840 --> 00:10:38.620
look like and what specialist centres will
look, so that we can feed that thought leadership

00:10:38.620 --> 00:10:44.500
into the local thinking that people are having
about the way in which services operate.

00:10:44.500 --> 00:10:52.010
We also plan to make an inspection around
the power and ability of information technology,

00:10:52.010 --> 00:10:56.980
both to save money, to improve outcomes, but
also to empower patients.

00:10:56.980 --> 00:11:02.300
We commissioned some work to finish in March,
which is going to set out what investments

00:11:02.300 --> 00:11:10.740
are required in information technology, but
more importantly what the benefits, both financially

00:11:10.740 --> 00:11:13.200
and in outcome terms, are to making that happen.

00:11:13.200 --> 00:11:21.340
Finally, we have commissioned some work around
the question: what is the monetary value -- what

00:11:21.340 --> 00:11:28.170
are the benefits to the financial position
of the NHS -- to giving patients more power

00:11:28.170 --> 00:11:31.730
and responsibility for their own health and
healthcare?

00:11:31.730 --> 00:11:36.101
We think if you do that, you can develop a
mechanism where people can start to think

00:11:36.101 --> 00:11:39.450
about how they can invest in that to make
it a reality.

00:11:39.450 --> 00:11:46.630
So these, I think, are important interventions,
as well as the work we need to do on specialised

00:11:46.630 --> 00:11:48.820
commissioning and primary care, which we have
commissioned as well.

00:11:48.820 --> 00:11:53.590
We think that when we add all that together,
that is a really powerful way of supporting

00:11:53.590 --> 00:12:10.350
and helping local CCGs and local organisations
develop their plans for 

00:12:10.350 --> 00:12:11.350
the future.

00:12:11.350 --> 00:12:29.940
This is a key point for us.

00:12:29.940 --> 00:12:34.140
We hope in future this process will be significantly
streamlined.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:41.750
However, just to reiterate to the board, one
of the key issues for us is the affordability

00:12:41.750 --> 00:12:43.480
of the mandate whatever happens.

00:12:43.480 --> 00:12:49.290
So we need to be absolutely clear and transparent
about what the financial consequences are

00:12:49.290 --> 00:12:52.190
for any of the things that we do in these
circumstances.

00:12:52.190 --> 00:12:58.380
We need to be absolutely clear with both government
and the public around how much money there

00:12:58.380 --> 00:13:08.640
is and what we can actually deliver for it.

00:13:08.640 --> 00:13:14.110
Victor Adebowale: You were talking about bringing
people together to talk about the future services.

00:13:14.110 --> 00:13:24.170
I am assuming that you are talking about people
from outside the NHS, not the usual family.

00:13:24.170 --> 00:13:28.340
David Nicholson: Are you talking about the
providers?

00:13:28.340 --> 00:13:29.470
Victor Adebowale: Yes.

00:13:29.470 --> 00:13:37.280
David Nicholson: Yes, there are people from
outside as well as inside the NHS, and internationally

00:13:37.280 --> 00:13:43.170
to kind of feed into the thought leadership
around what the provision in the future might

00:13:43.170 --> 00:13:44.170
look like.

00:13:44.170 --> 00:13:45.170
Yes, we are.

00:13:45.170 --> 00:13:46.170
Malcolm Grant: Thank you very much, David.

00:13:46.170 --> 00:13:53.440
I think on the mandate we have reached a point
now where we have something that is capable

00:13:53.440 --> 00:13:54.950
of being agreed with the department.

00:13:54.950 --> 00:14:00.730
The struggle has always been between trying
to ensure that the mandate, which is a fundamental

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:06.910
document around which we can base our future
planning is sufficiently visionary and forward

00:14:06.910 --> 00:14:11.180
looking, and picks up the themes that David
has been referring to earlier, in his presentation

00:14:11.180 --> 00:14:15.790
this morning, and is not simply a shopping
list of everything government departments

00:14:15.790 --> 00:14:18.660
might wish to see imposed upon us.

00:14:18.660 --> 00:14:23.570
So I think we have now got the balance better
than we might have thought possible at one

00:14:23.570 --> 00:14:29.810
stage, but the critical thing, as David has
said, is still ensuring that the financial

00:14:29.810 --> 00:14:35.430
consequences of enhanced commitments in the
mandate are fully picked up and appreciated.

00:14:35.430 --> 00:14:39.860
So, I am grateful for David and the team for
ensuring that is kept at the forefront of

00:14:39.860 --> 00:14:40.779
everybody's minds.

